2026-06-30 15:38:05,626 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-ee8ed273-e7ad-4160-a83e-5f1ae6130c1c', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: sex\nsdrf_column: characteristics[sex]\nontology: PATO:0000047\ndescription: Biological sex of the donor.\nallowed_values: male, female, intersex, hermaphrodite, anonymized, not available, not applicable\ninstructions: Use one of the fixed values above. Return "not available" if not stated.\n  Return "not applicable" if the sample is from a cell line or organism without defined sex.\n\nterm: age\nsdrf_column: characteristics[age]\nontology: EFO:0000246\ndescription: Age of the donor at sample collection.\npattern: {Number}{Unit} where Unit is Y (years), M (months), W (weeks), D (days).\n  Also allowed: "anonymized" if age is stated but anonymized.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: ancestry category\nsdrf_column: characteristics[ancestry category]\nontology: HANCESTRO\ndescription: Ancestry or ethnic background of the donor.\ninstructions: Use HANCESTRO terms if possible. Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: biological replicate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[biological replicate]\nontology: MS:1001809\ndescription: Identifier for the biological replicate (integer or "pooled").\ninstructions: Return a description of the replication strategy from the paper\n  (e.g. "3 biological replicates", "2 independent experiments"). The actual\n  integer values are assigned per-file automatically; describe what you find in the text.\n\nterm: genotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genotype]\nontology: EFO:0000513\ndescription: Known genetic variant, mutation, or genotype of the subject.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Example: "wild type", "Δatg5", "ΔYme1".\n\nterm: pooled sample\nsdrf_column: characteristics[pooled sample]\nontology: NCIT:C165587\ndescription: Whether the sample is a pooled sample.\nallowed_values: not pooled, pooled, or pattern SN=sample1;SN=sample2\ninstructions: Return "not pooled" unless explicitly stated otherwise.\n\nterm: phosphate\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phosphate]\nontology: PRIDE:0000805\ndescription: Phosphate concentration (only relevant for environmental/water samples).\npattern: number + mg/L or umol/L\ninstructions: Return null unless this is a water/environmental sample with explicit\n  phosphate concentration stated.\n\nterm: temperature\nsdrf_column: characteristics[temperature]\nontology: PRIDE:0000703\ndescription: Temperature at the sampling location (environmental samples only).\npattern: number + °C\ninstructions: Return null unless this is an environmental/metaproteomics sample\n  with explicit temperature stated. Do NOT confuse with crosslinking temperature.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nHsp70 and NAC chaperone systems are in close proximity on the ribosome\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nTo understand the orientation of Hsp70 Ssb on the ribosome in living Saccharomyces cerevisiae cells we incorporated the noncanonical, photoactivatable amino acid p-benzoyl-L-phenylalanine (Bpa) in place of specific individual endogenous amino acids in Ssb1. To identify the proteins to which Ssb1Bpa crosslinked we performed mass spectrometry. The results revealed that Bpa incorporated at the tip of the “lid” subdomain cross linked to the nascent chain associated complex (NAC), while Bpa incorporated into further down the lid crosslinked to ribosomal protein uL29.\n\n[METHODS]\nAll yeast strains to be used for in vivo crosslinking were transformed with plasmid ptRNA-Bpa, which directs incorporation of p-benzoyl-L-phenylalanine (Bpa) at TAG codons, and a plasmid having a TAG mutation at the desired position for Bpa incorporation in the target protein. Strains were then grown on the appropriate selective minimal medium with 0.4 mM Bpa, starting at an OD600nm between 0.03 and 0.10. At OD600nm = 0.5-1.0, cycloheximide was added to 0.1 mg/ml and cells harvested by centrifugation. 12.5-25 OD600nm units of resuspended cells were divided in half - one half subjected to 365 nm UV illumination for 1 hr at 4˚C (Stratalinker 1800 UV crosslinker with 365 nm bulbs); the other was kept on ice as a control. Cells were lysed by agitation with glass beads for 5 min at 4˚C in lysis buffer (300 mM sorbitol, 20 mM HEPES-KOH, pH 7.5, 1 mM EGTA, 5 mM MgCl2, 10 mM KCl, 10% glycerol v/v, 1 mM dithiothreitol and RNasin RNase Inhibitor (Promega) at a dilution of 1:1,000). Lysates were clarified by centrifugation at 16,100 rcf for 10 min in a microcentrifuge (Eppendorf). Typically, 5 Abs260 units of cleared cell lysate was loaded onto a 0.8 ml sucrose cushion composed of lysis buffer having 0.5 M sucrose instead of sorbitol. To pellet ribosomes the cell extracts were centrifuged for 2 hr at 150,700 rcf in a MLA130 rotor (Beckman Coulter) at 4˚C. The pellet was suspended in SDS sample buffer (0.124 M Tris-HCl buffer, pH 6.8, 4% w/v SDS, 10% v/v glycerol, 0.02% bromophenol blue, 4.5% b-mercaptoethanol) prior to SDS-PAGE. Resuspended sucrose cushion pellets were resolved on 7.5% SDS-PAGE, followed by staining with either Coomassie brilliant blue or PierceTM (Thermo Scientific) silver stain for mass spectrometry. After band excision and destaining, samples were alkylated with iodoacetic acid prior to digestion with trypsin and solid phase extracted as described previously (Gamerdinger, 2019). Resulting peptides were analyzed by nanoLC-MS/MS using the Agilent 1100 nanoflow system (Agilent) connected to a hybrid linear ion trap-orbitrap mass spectrometer (LTQ-Orbitrap Elite™, Thermo Fisher Scientific) equipped with an EASY-Spray™ electrospray source (Gamerdinger, 2019).\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
